   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription Delhi Chief Minister Rekha Gupta on Saturday inaugurated the Delhi Pavilion at the 45th India International Trade Fair (IITF-2026), being held at Bharat Mandapam. “The Delhi Pavilion brings together the Capital’s glorious heritage and its journey of modern development. According to a Delhi government statement, the pavilion showcases the city’s art, culture, historical identity and heritage, along with initiatives such as Namo Van, Swachh Yamuna and the promotion of electric vehicles.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁这些内容：德里首席部长雷卡·古普塔周六为在 Bharat Mandapam 举行的第45届印度国际贸易博览会（IITF-2026）内的德里展馆揭幕。“德里展馆荟萃了这座首都辉煌的传承及其现代化发展之路。”德里政府声明称，展馆展示了该市的艺术、文化、历史身份和遗产，以及 Namo Van、Swachh Yamuna 和推广电动汽车等举措。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Delhi Chief Minister Rekha Gupta on Saturday inaugurated the Delhi Pavilion at the 45th India International Trade Fair (IITF-2026), being held at Bharat Mandapam. Delhi has been designated the Focus State at this year’s fair. Ms. Gupta said the opportunity was being used to showcase the Capital’s growing economic, cultural and industrial identity. “The Delhi Pavilion brings together the Capital’s glorious heritage and its journey of modern development. It also provides entrepreneurs, women business owners, artisans and local producers with an opportunity to showcase their talent and products and gain wider recognition,” she said. She added that the Delhi government aimed to preserve the Capital’s cultural identity while highlighting its work in development, innovation, entrepreneurship and environmental conservation at national and international levels. Spread over 996 sq. metres, the pavilion features 62 stalls, 10 of which have been allocated to women entrepreneurs. According to a Delhi government statement, the pavilion showcases the city’s art, culture, historical identity and heritage, along with initiatives such as Namo Van, Swachh Yamuna and the promotion of electric vehicles. “Through these displays, visitors will get to know about the development works underway in Delhi, the efforts being made towards environmental conservation and the Capital’s future development priorities,” the statement said. After visiting the stalls and interacting with participants, Ms. Gupta said Delhi was not only the national Capital but also a symbol of India’s rich historical heritage, diverse culture, entrepreneurship and modern development. “Through the Delhi Pavilion, this multifaceted identity of the Capital is being showcased on national and international platforms,” she said. IITF 2026 will run from November 13 to 27 across 17 halls at Bharat Mandapam under the theme ‘Vikas aur Virasat’. Business-only days will continue until November 18. The fair has brought together more than 3,500 exhibitors from India and abroad across over 1 lakh sq. metres of exhibition space. It will see participation from 32 States and Union Territories and 51 Central Ministries, government departments, public sector undertakings and public sector banks. International participation includes 11 countries, with pavilions featuring exhibitors from Thailand, Iran, Ghana, Nepal, Afghanistan, the UAE, Turkey, China, Sri Lanka and Bangladesh, providing opportunities for business engagement and trade linkages. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
