   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription Bangladesh has completed all legal procedures required to secure the return of ousted Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina from India, and the next step rests with New Delhi, Law Minister Md Asaduzzaman said on Saturday (November 14, 2026), according to local media reports. Editorial | In a dilemma: On Sheikh Hasina and India-Bangladesh ties “We have completed everything required from our side as part of the legal process.",
   "summary_zh": "使用订阅解锁这些内容 据当地媒体报道，孟加拉国司法部长阿萨杜扎曼周六（2026年11月14日）表示，该国已完成确保被罢免总理谢赫·哈西娜从印度返回所需的全部法律程序，下一步取决于新德里。社论｜陷入两难：谈谢赫·哈西娜与孟印关系 “作为法律程序的一部分，我们已完成我方所需的一切工作。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Bangladesh has completed all legal procedures required to secure the return of ousted Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina from India, and the next step rests with New Delhi, Law Minister Md Asaduzzaman said on Saturday (November 14, 2026), according to local media reports. Editorial | In a dilemma: On Sheikh Hasina and India-Bangladesh ties “We have completed everything required from our side as part of the legal process. What they [India] will do is up to them,” Mr. Asaduzzaman told reporters after an event at the Bangladesh-China Friendship Conference Centre in Dhaka, according to Prothom Alo . The Minister said Ms. Hasina would return through legal means and her extradition under the bilateral treaty would itself constitute a legal process. Regarding whether she would be entitled to appeal her death sentence, Mr. Asaduzzaman said she would not have that opportunity under the relevant law, Dhaka Tribune reported. When asked whether India had discussed sending Ms. Hasina to a third country with Bangladesh, the Minister said the Indian government would be able to answer that question, Prothom Alo reported. Bangladesh has been seeking Ms. Hasina’s extradition from India after she fled the country in August 2024 following a student-led uprising that toppled her government. Bangladesh’s International Crimes Tribunal convicted Ms. Hasina in November 2025 for her role in the crackdown on protesters during the uprising. Tried in absentia, she was sentenced to death on one charge and life imprisonment on another. Ms. Hasina has rejected the charges and the proceedings. Bangladesh’s demand for Ms. Hasina’s extradition has continued even after the interim government that succeeded her administration handed over power in February to an elected government led by Prime Minister Tarique Rahman. India has acknowledged receiving Bangladesh’s extradition requests but has not announced a decision on handing her over. On Friday (November 13), Ministry of External Affairs spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal said in New Delhi that Ms. Hasina’s extradition was a legal matter and would be dealt with accordingly. Ms. Hasina has said she intends to return to Bangladesh in December. She made the announcement during an audio-only virtual press conference with journalists organised in New Delhi on August 5. The event drew a sharp reaction from Dhaka; Bangladesh’s Foreign Ministry said it was “outraged” that Ms. Hasina had been allowed to interact with the media in New Delhi despite Dhaka conveying its concerns to the Indian government in advance. India, however, distanced itself from the event, saying it was organised by a private media entity and that the government had “no involvement whatsoever” in it. Dhaka has also linked progress on Ms. Hasina’s extradition to a possible visit to India by Mr. Rahman, saying New Delhi should expedite her handover and return other fugitives sought by Bangladesh to create a conducive environment for the visit. The India-Bangladesh consular dialogue was scheduled for next week, but it has been postponed. Ms. Hasina has no legal option left but to face execution of her death sentence, the International Crimes Tribunal’s chief prosecutor Md. Aminul Islam said earlier this week, rejecting the possibility of issuing her a passport or travel document. His remarks came a day after Ms. Hasina, through a London-based legal chamber, approached the Bangladesh High Commission in New Delhi seeking a new passport and national identity card. Bangladesh’s Home Minister Salahuddin Ahmed had then said the government was examining the authenticity and legal grounds of applications submitted by Ms. Hasina. However, Bangladesh’s State Minister for Foreign Affairs Humaiun Kobir reiterated that Dhaka would seek her return from India only through an existing extradition treaty. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
